:answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418501} :answer-field/key "field-16" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418536} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418536} :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418728}] :question/math-academy-id "q-183943" :question/problem "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=7{a}_{n}-10{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=21,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2\n$$\n\nuse strong induction to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n},\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-8}} for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ for all {{field-9}} for some {{field-10}}. In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={{field-11}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: Using the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =({{field-13}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}_{k}-10{a}_{k-1} & =7({{field-14}})-10({{field-15}}) \\\\  & =5({{field-16}})-2({{field-17}}) \\\\  & ={{field-18}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ is true for all {{field-19}}."}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Explicit Formulas for Second-Order Recurrence Relations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049290 :topic/math-academy-id 712 :topic/title "Strong Induction and Recurrence Relations"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083200 :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235832} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235852} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236871} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237049} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236871} :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237745}] :question/math-academy-id "q-100495" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285384} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285521} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286344} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286697} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2"